=== layout: section

# Summary


=== layout: one-panel

# Mendel's Four Principles of Heredity

@body

- [*Paired factors*]{underline=true}: diploid organisms have two alleles at a locus.
- [*Dominance*]{underline=true}: in complete dominance, the dominant allele determines the heterozygote phenotype.
- [*Segregation*]{underline=true}: each gamete receives one allele of each pair.
- [*Independent assortment*]{underline=true}: unlinked genes assort independently.

- Exceptions: [NOT ALL ORGANISMS ARE DIPLOID]{color=#CC00CC}; dominance can be [INCOMPLETE or CODOMINANT]{color=#CC00CC}; genes can interact ([EPISTASIS]{color=#CC00CC}).
- Males are hemizygous for most X-linked genes: one copy, rather than a pair.
- A mammalian father passes his X chromosome to daughters and his Y to sons.

